 if a hosted endpoint is used; interleaving and per-item medians mitigate but do not eliminate this.", "Whether MGSM must be run on the full 2,500 items or a stratified per-language subsample (cost vs precision, unresolved).", "Whether any compute/backend exists at all for a real run; the present sandbox has no model, no data, no dependencies and no network, so a live execution would require resources outside it."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-df1855fb-22c6-44fd-8607-cbe0b02095d5", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1. Input inspection \u2014 what is actually in the working area\n\n**Present.** `/workspace` contains exactly one file:\n- `task.json` (518 bytes). Contents: the question string; a `context` field (\"Design an experiment ... Do not claim that an experiment was run\"); and `documents.track.md` = *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"*\n\n**Absent (verified by filesystem search, not assumed).**\n- No BoT paper/PDF/TXT anywhere on the mounted filesystem (searched `/` to depth 6 for `*buffer*thought*`, `*96264*`, `*.pdf`, `*.tex`, `*.md` outside `/usr`,`/proc`,`/lib`: zero hits).\n- No code for Buffer of Thoughts, Expert prompting, or PAL. No baseline prompt files, no retrieval index, no thought-template buffer, no embedding artefacts.\n- No task suites: no Game of 24 puzzles, no MGSM files, no Checkmate-in-One dataset (`task.json` itself has `\"datasets\": []`).\n- No model weights, no API keys/credentials, no environment configuration pointing at an inference endpoint.\n\n**Runnability.** Nothing is runnable end-to-end. Python 3.14.4 exists, but of `numpy, pandas, scipy, torch, transformers, openai, datasets, sklearn, nltk, sympy, tqdm, sentence_transformers, faiss, rank_bm25` only `requests` imports; the others are MISSING. `pip` (25.1.1) is present but **network is disabled** (DNS resolution fails), so dependencies cannot be installed. `gpu_enabled: false`. So even a local open-weight model plus a retriever could not be built here.\nWhat *is* available, and is sufficient for the design-only scope: a monotonic high-resolution clock (`time.perf_counter_ns`, verified \u2014 10.189 ms sleep measured as 10.189 ms), `statistics` (median/quantiles), and `resource`.\n\n**Explicit statement of scope.** This is a **design-only exercise**. I have executed **no** experiment and I will report **nothing as run**. The only commands I ran are filesystem/environment feasibility probes; per the task's own note, successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment, and they are not experimental outcomes here.\n\n## 2. Objects of comparison \u2014 confirmation and gaps\n\n- **Confirmed** from the supplied question string: the method under examination is **Buffer of Thoughts** (\"the retrieval-augmented, thought/template-guided reasoning framework\"), and the required single-query comparators are **Expert Prompting** and **Program-Aided LLM (PAL)**, on **Game of 24, MGSM, and Checkmate-in-One**.\n- **Caveat 1 (comparator closure).** The wording is \"single-query reasoning methods **including** Expert and PAL\", which does not exhaustively close the set. I propose Expert + PAL as the *only* two required comparators, with **CoT added as an unrequired internal reference arm** (cheap, and it anchors the absolute latency scale). Your call.\n- **Caveat 2 (coverage asymmetry \u2014 consequential).** In the source material, Expert prompting and PAL are not obviously evaluated on all three suites (Expert is naturally associated with the chess task, PAL with the arithmetic/code tasks). Whether all nine (method \u00d7 benchmark) cells must be populated, or only the cells the source reports, materially changes both scope and whether the conclusion can be stated across \"various benchmarks\". Flagged as decision D1 below.\n- **Specification gaps.** The supplied material specifies **nothing** about: the base LLM(s) or their version pins; decoding settings (temperature, top-p, `max_tokens`, stop sequences); or the BoT retrieval store (embedding model, index/ann type, k, similarity metric, buffer contents and size, whether the buffer is per-benchmark or global). **All are left open** and must be either pinned from the paper (via your `setup_7/8/9/28/29` topics \u2014 I cannot read them; the paper text is not on disk) or fixed by decree as a design choice. I recommend the former wherever possible and have listed the exact fields to extract (section 5).\n\n## 3. Proposed approach, in outline only (full design withheld pending your approval)\n\n**A. Timed quantity (primary endpoint).** Per-item, per-method end-to-end wall-clock latency `T(m, i)`, measured with `perf_counter_ns`, defined as *prompt-assembly-complete \u2192 final answer accepted (parsed and validated against the task checker)*, under fixed conditions: concurrency = 1 request in flight, no client-side batching, one dedicated process, single API endpoint/region or one self-hosted pinned checkpoint, first `W` items per condition discarded as warm-up.\n\n**B. Component decomposition (secondary endpoint, still timed).** Log per item: embedding time, retrieval/template-selection time, LLM decode time, program-execution time (PAL, and BoT when its template emits code), parse/validate time, retry time, plus token counts and LLM-call counts. Verify components sum to `T` within tolerance; this is what makes the comparison interpretable rather than a black-box number.\n\n**C. Excluded from `T`, reported separately.** Retrieval-store/buffer construction and embedding-index build are reported as a **one-time setup cost and as an amortised per-query charge** (build time \u00f7 N items), never folded into the primary latency endpoint and never silently dropped.\n\n**D. Benchmarks and sampling.** Fixed, pre-registered, seeded item set, **identical for all methods** (fully paired design). Game of 24: the full published puzzle set; Checkmate-in-One: the full released set; MGSM: 250 items/language \u00d7 10 languages, with a pre-registered stratified subsample (e.g. 50/language, seeded) if cost demands \u2014 subsample size fixed before any measurement.\n\n**E. Repetitions and ordering.** `R` repeats per (item, method) \u2014 proposed R = 5 \u2014 even under greedy decoding, because API-side nondeterminism and load variance make a single timing unreliable. Conditions **interleaved in randomised order** (never \"all BoT then all PAL\") to break time-of-day drift; fixed cool-down between items. Per-item summary = **median across the R repeats** (robust to API spikes); IQR retained. Seeds recorded for item sampling, ordering, and decoding.\n\n**F. Accuracy held alongside latency.** Correctness is recorded from the *same* runs (no separate accuracy pass). Derived endpoint: **time-to-one-correct-answer = mean latency \u00f7 accuracy**, with a bootstrap CI over items; plus accuracy\u2013latency Pareto display and \"wall-clock time to first success across R attempts\". This makes a cost-per-correct-answer claim possible rather than a raw speed claim.\n\n**G. Statistical treatment.** Paired analysis on **per-item log-latency ratios** `log T(BoT,i) \u2212 log T(baseline,i)`: paired Wilcoxon signed-rank for the headline p-value, one-sided for \"increase\"; **cluster bootstrap over items** (clustering the R repeats) for CIs on the median ratio; **Holm\u2013Bonferroni** across the 6 method-pair \u00d7 benchmark comparisons. Crucially, add a **pre-registered TOST equivalence test** with a practical margin \u03b4 (proposed \u00b110% median latency), so the design can *support* \"BoT does not increase inference time\" instead of merely failing to reject an increase \u2014 otherwise a null result is uninterpretable. Effect sizes (median ratio) reported alongside every p-value; exploratory regression of log-latency on output tokens to separate \"fewer tokens\" from \"cheaper per token\".\n\n**Dictated by the source material:** the three benchmarks, Expert/PAL as single-query comparators, and (subject to your topic lookups) base model, decoding settings and retrieval-store configuration. **My own proposed choices:** R = 5, per-item median, concurrency = 1, warm-up exclusion, interleaved ordering, log-ratio paired tests, cluster bootstrap, Holm correction, the \u03b4 = 10% TOST margin, the amortised-setup convention, and time-to-one-correct-answer as the joint endpoint.\n\n## 4. Consequential assumptions (proposed defaults \u2014 need your assent)\n\n- **A1 Caching.** Retrieval index and template buffer **pre-built once and cached** across the test set; BoT charged only per-query lookup. (Charging build per query would unfairly inflate BoT; hiding it entirely would unfairly deflate it \u2014 hence C.)\n- **A2 Hardware/concurrency held constant.** Same machine, same endpoint/region, same pinned model version, same process, concurrency = 1 for all three. If an API is used instead of a self-hosted checkpoint, day-to-day latency variance becomes the dominant noise source \u2014 the strongest threat to the conclusion, mitigated (not removed) by interleaving and medians.\n- **A3 Identical output-token budget** (`max_tokens`, stop rules) for all three; token counts reported, on the position that template-driven brevity is part of BoT's claimed mechanism, not a confound to be removed.\n- **A4 Overheads measured for baselines too** \u2014 PAL's Python execution and parsing, and Expert's prose-answer extraction, are inside `T`, exactly as BoT's retrieval is.\n- **A5 Identical retry/error policy**, with retries *included* in the primary endpoint (a template-selection failure that forces a fallback LLM call is a real cost) and *excluded* in a sensitivity analysis.\n- **A6 Strictly single-query for all arms at k = 1.** Any self-consistency / k-vote variant (notably reported for Game of 24) is a labelled secondary condition only, never mixed into the primary paired comparison.\n- **A7 MGSM uses one multilingual-capable retriever** across all 10 languages, with per-language latency reported (retrieval cost is language-dependent).\n\n**Uncertainties.** (i) **What \"inference time\" is meant to cover** \u2014 LLM forward pass only, or the whole pipeline including embedding, retrieval, sandboxed execution, parsing and retries. This is the largest definitional risk; my default is end-to-end wall-clock with decomposition reported. (ii) Whether all nine method \u00d7 benchmark cells are answerable, or only those the source evaluates. (iii) Whether the source's reported timings used a batch API, a different concurrency, or an unpinned model version, which would make our numbers non-comparable to the paper's. (iv) Whether \"increase\" is to be judged on mean, median or tail latency \u2014 I propose median as primary, P90 secondary, and this must be fixed before any measurement. (v) MGSM full 2,500 items vs. a stratified subsample is a cost/precision trade-off left open.\n\n## 5. What I need from you before drafting the full design\n\nSix topic queries are available; I propose spending them on: (1) base LLM name(s) + version pins used for all three methods; (2) decoding settings and `max_tokens`; (3) BoT retrieval-store configuration (embedder, index, k, similarity, buffer scope/size, per-benchmark or global); (4) the paper's own inference-time measurement protocol (what was inside the timer, hardware/API, concurrency, warm-up); (5) benchmark sizes/splits and whether Expert and PAL are each evaluated on all three suites; (6) any reported absolute timings/accuracy that our design should aim to reproduce as a sanity anchor. I will not draft the full design, controls, analysis plan and feasibility discussion until you approve the above approach and settle A1\u2013A7 and decisions D1 (benchmark coverage) and D2 (add CoT as reference arm?).", "evidence": "Filesystem and environment probes executed in the task sandbox (feasibility checks only; no 