{"id": "f6089c68-b0e0-4eb0-bcbd-464950fa6660", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T10:27:07.460913", "source": "user", "parent_id": "4d5c5dca-6612-49cb-9613-e0f17c916674", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "The approach is sound and I approve it. You have been disciplined about scope: nothing was run, nothing is claimed as run, and the filesystem and environment probes are reported as feasibility checks only. I am content to proceed to the full design on the following settled terms. Please treat my additions below as the collaborator's direction, not as source facts.\n\nSettled decisions.\n\nD1, benchmark coverage. Populate the full three-by-three grid (Expert, PAL, BoT on Game of 24, MGSM, Checkmate-in-One) as the design's target. Where a comparator is arguably ill-fitted to a suite, do not drop the cell: keep it, mark it with an explicit applicability note, and pre-register how a non-applicable cell is handled in the multiplicity correction. The question is phrased across \"various benchmarks\", so a design that silently answers only a subset is weaker than one that states coverage honestly.\n\nD2, reference arm. Yes, include chain-of-thought as a clearly labelled unrequired internal reference arm. It anchors the absolute latency scale cheaply, but it must never enter the headline paired contrasts or the Holm family, which stays at the Expert and PAL comparisons.\n\nAssumptions A1 to A7. I assent to all seven as drafted. Three remarks of emphasis. On A1 and C together: the amortised per-query charge for buffer and index construction is the right compromise, and I would ask you to also report the break-even item count at which amortised construction exceeds per-query lookup, as it makes the caching convention transparent rather than convenient. On A2: I accept that API variance is the dominant threat and that interleaving plus per-item medians mitigate rather than remove it; please state plainly in the feasibility section that a pinned self-hosted checkpoint is the preferred backend and that hosted-endpoint results are weaker evidence. On A3: I agree that template-driven brevity is part of BoT's claimed mechanism and not a confound to be engineered away; the token-count regression you propose is the correct way to separate \"fewer tokens\" from \"cheaper per token\".\n\nDefinitional rulings, since you asked me to fix these.\n\n(i) \"Inference time\" is hereby fixed as end-to-end per-item wall-clock latency for every arm, inclusive of embedding, retrieval, program execution, parsing and validation, with the component decomposition reported alongside. (ii) The authoritative statistic is the median paired ratio, with P90 as the secondary tail view; the mean is descriptive only. (iii) The TOST margin of plus or minus ten per cent is accepted as the primary practical-equivalence margin, on condition that you justify it explicitly against the reported accuracy gains, and add a sensitivity analysis at five per cent. If a ten per cent margin cannot be defended, say so rather than asserting it.\n\nOne firm requirement on source grounding. The five setup items I can see confirm only the identity of the comparators: Expert Prompting and PAL as single-query baselines, BoT as retrieval-augmented template-guided reasoning, and the same pairing stated again later in the section. They say nothing about base model, version pins, decoding settings, max_tokens, or the retrieval store. You are correct that these are open. I cannot read more than what has been disclosed to me, and I will not supply them from memory as if they were paper facts. Therefore the full design must present every such field either as a named, pinned design choice declared by us, or as an explicitly unresolved field to be extracted from the paper before execution. Do not attribute them to the source.\n\nLikewise, treat item counts for Game of 24, MGSM and Checkmate-in-One as design parameters to be declared, not as verified dataset facts, since no data is present on disk. The MGSM subsample size should be fixed before measurement as you propose, and the full-versus-subsample choice stated as a cost-precision trade-off with a defensible default.\n\nDeliverable I now want. The complete experiment design: rationale; arms and cell coverage including the applicability notes; the timed quantity and decomposition; item sampling and pairing; repeats, ordering and warm-up; the accuracy-latency joint endpoint; the statistical plan including the paired log-ratio test, cluster bootstrap, Holm family definition, and TOST with its margin justification; the pre-registered sensitivity analyses (retries excluded, concurrency varied, subsample size varied, five per cent margin); the feasibility assessment against this sandbox's actual limits, namely no model, no data, no dependencies, no network, no accelerator, and what resources a real run would require; and an explicit statement that no experiment was executed and that no expected outcome is asserted. Keep it itemised and precise.", "required_handoff_stage": "result", "approach_approved": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "21c3b4e4-f2ca-477d-be42-c4e447b8ea09", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T10:28:22.996177", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "f6089c68-b0e0-4eb0-bcbd-464950fa6660", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved the approach and wants the complete design. I should do a few more feasibility checks to ground the design (e.g., verify stdlib capabilities for bootstrap/permutation tests, check timing overhead, maybe estimate computational feasibility). Then produce the full design with stage=result.

Let me do some quick feasibility probes that are genuinely useful:
1. Verify stdlib can do Wilcoxon signed-rank (can implement manually), bootstrap (numpy absent but random module fine).
2. Measure perf_counter_ns resolution and clock overhead per measurement.
3. Check whether a sandboxed Python executor for PAL-style code could be built with stdlib (resource limits, subprocess) — relevant to feasibility.
4. Check a chess move generator availability — Checkmate-in-One validation requires python-chess, absent; but could note we need a validator. Maybe check if we can implement minimal FEN parsing... that's a feasibility item worth checking briefly.
5. Check Game of 24 verifier with fractions (stdlib `fractions`) — feasible.

These are legitimately design-informing checks. Let me run them in parallel.

Also I should think about the TOST margin justification: ±10% must be justified against reported accuracy gains — but accuracy gains are not available from source (unresolved field). So I must say: the margin justification depends on the accuracy delta which is an unresolved source field; therefore I pre-register a decision-theoretic rule: margin δ set so that time-per-correct-answer is not worse... Actually a principled approach: define margin in terms of cost-per-correct-answer: if BoT gains Δ accuracy points, then a latency increase up to ratio ρ_Acc/(ρ_Acc+Δ) keeps time-to-correct no worse. E.g., if BoT accuracy = 97% vs Expert 90% on Checkmate-in-One, then a 10% latency increase (ratio 1.10) yields BoT time-per-correct = 1.10/97 = 0.01134 vs 1/90 = 0.01111 → marginally worse; breakeven latency ratio = 97/90 = 1.078. So a ±10% latency margin is only defensible if the accuracy gain is large enough that the latency-increase case still ties or wins on cost-per-correct-answer, OR if the practical question is pure latency.

Better: justify ±10% via the accuracy-conditional break-even: δ = 10% is defensible iff, for every cell, the accuracy gain Δ satisfies Δ/(Acc_bot) ≥ ... hmm. Let me formalize.

Time-to-one-correct-answer: TTA = median_latency / accuracy. BoT is no-worse on TTA iff L_bot/L_base ≤ Acc_bot/Acc_base. So the acceptable latency ratio is R* = Acc_bot/Acc_base. If Acc_bot = Acc_base (no gain), R* = 1.0 → even a 1% latency increase makes BoT worse on cost-per-correct. If Acc_bot = 0.97, Acc_base = 0.90, R* = 1.078. If Acc_bot = 0.99, Acc_base=0.94, R*=1.053.

So: a ±10% TOST margin on latency is defensible ONLY if accuracy gains ≥ ~9–10% relative in every cell; otherwise, within the cost-per-correct framing, a 10% latency increase IS a practical worsening. Since accuracy gains are unresolved source fields (paper reports high accuracy for BoT; e.g., near-ceiling on Game of 24 — but I cannot attribute to source), I must state: the ±10% margin is defensible as a pure-latency practicality margin (i.e., a stakeholder who treats latency as the sole endpoint would not act on a <10% median difference, which is within the run-to-run/diurnal variance of a hosted endpoint — that's an empirical justification tied to measurement noise), but it is NOT defensible as implying "no cost penalty" unless accuracy parity is established. Hence: primary TOST at ±10% on the latency endpoint, plus the joint endpoint (TTA) TOST at ±0% (i.e., non-inferiority on TTA at margin 0 with a two-stage logic), plus sensitivity at ±5%. And a pre-registered rule: if the measured accuracy deltas, once known, imply R* < 1.10 in some cell, that cell's headline verdict is reported on the TTA endpoint and the latency-only verdict is downgraded to descriptive.

Also, I can justify 10% by a noise-floor argument: measure test–retest variability of the same method on an anchor subset; if the retest median ratio deviates by >5% routinely, then a 5% margin is within the measurement noise floor and cannot be resolved — this is a defensible, empirical way to set the margin. Pre-register: margin must exceed 2× the observed anchor test-retest SD of log-ratio, else the design is under-powered for that margin. That's a strong, honest treatment: "If a 10% margin cannot be defended, say so." Actually the researcher asked to justify 10% against reported accuracy gains, add sensitivity at 5%. I'll present: (a) noise-floor argument (10% is defensible if noise floor below ~5%, 5% margin defensible only on a pinned self-hosted backend), (b) accuracy-equivalence argument giving the break-even ratio R*, with a pre-registered table across plausible accuracy pairs (explicitly labelled as hypothetical, not paper facts).

Break-even item count for amortised construction: N* such that build_time/N* = per-query lookup time t_ret → N* = t_ret... no: amortised construction cost per item = B/N. Amortised construction exceeds per-query lookup when B/N > t_ret → N < B/t_ret. So the break-even count N* = B / t_ret: for benchmarks with fewer than N* items, construction dominates lookup; above it, lookup dominates. Report B, t_ret, and N* per benchmark. Good.

Statistical plan detail:
- Paired log-ratio per item i: d_i = log median_r T_bot(i,r) − log median_r T_base(i,r).
